parts.body:
New York lets an LTC policy exclude coverage only for a short list of reasons (11 NYCRR 52.25(b)(2)):

- preexisting conditions — only for up to **6 months** after the effective date, for conditions treated or advised within **6 months** before it;
- mental or nervous disorders (but **not** Alzheimer's disease or organic brain disease);
- alcoholism and drug addiction;
- war, felony/riot/insurrection, military service, suicide or self-inflicted injury, and aviation as a non-fare-paying passenger;
- care covered by Medicare or other government programs (except Medicaid), workers' comp, or no-fault; care by immediate family; care for which no charge is normally made;
- care outside the United States.

**Underwriting** happens at application. New York prohibits **post-claims underwriting** — waiting until a claim to investigate health history. Policies may not require a prior hospital stay before paying benefits.

**Suitability**: LTC insurance fits people with assets to protect who can keep paying premiums, which may rise on a class basis. Someone with very low income and few assets may be better served by Medicaid if care is needed.

Every individual LTC policy in New York must be **[[guaranteed-renewable|guaranteed renewable]]**: the insured can keep it in force by paying premiums on time, and the insurer can't change any provision — except that **premium rates may be revised on a class basis** (11 NYCRR 52.25(b)(1)).

Buyers receive a **required disclosure statement** (11 NYCRR 52.65) summarizing the policy. Among other things it must:

- explain the free-look (right to return) provision;
- state **'THIS IS NOT MEDICARE SUPPLEMENT INSURANCE'**;
- describe benefits (LTC insurance is designed to provide at least **24 consecutive months** of coverage, including all levels of nursing home care and home care) and state that Alzheimer's disease is covered;
- describe limitations and exclusions;
- state that premiums may increase with the Superintendent's approval, show a 10-year premium rate history, and promise at least **90 days' notice** of any premium change;
- describe nonforfeiture benefits.

The disclosure statement is a summary — **not** the contract.

New York LTC rules prohibit, among other things:

- **post-claims underwriting**;
- requiring a **prior hospitalization** or prior level of care, an acute condition, or Medicare-certified providers before paying benefits;
- excluding **Alzheimer's** or organic brain disease.

**Replacement** (11 NYCRR 52.29): the application must ask whether the new policy will replace other accident and health coverage and must list existing policies. The producer signs a statement that the replacement or additional coverage is **appropriate for the applicant's needs**. When a sale involves replacement, the applicant must receive a **notice regarding replacement** before the policy is issued or delivered; the insurer keeps a copy the applicant has signed. The notice warns about new preexisting-condition limits and possibly higher premiums.

**Compensation**: an insurer may pay a higher first-year commission than renewal commission, but **in a replacement, compensation may not exceed the replacing insurer's renewal compensation** (11 NYCRR 52.25(e)(3)).
